      "id": "tendency-tones-and-resolution",
      "title": "Tendency Tones and Resolution",
      "level": 1,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "**Track:** harmony | **Difficulty:** 4/10 | **Time:** ~10 min"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "overview",
      "title": "Overview",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "Certain pitches in tonal music 'want' to move to others, creating the feeling of arrival or suspense."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "leading-tone",
      "title": "Leading tone",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "7th scale degree in major rises to tonic by semitone. In G major, F♯ wants to resolve up to G."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "tritone-resolution",
      "title": "Tritone resolution",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "The tritone in V7 (B and F in G7→C) resolves outward: B up to C, F down to E. This defines dominant function."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "melodic-tendency",
      "title": "Melodic tendency",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "2̂ often steps to 1̂ or 3̂. 4̂ may fall to 3̂. Backing musicians who clash with unresolved melody tendency sound wrong even on 'correct' chords."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
